<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Harry Johnston</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1858">1858</a>–<a href="/years/1927">1927</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Sir Harry Johnston</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir Harry Johnston was a British explorer and colonial administrator. As vice-consul in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-delta">Niger Delta</a> he deported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaja-of-opobo">King Jaja</a> of Opobo in 1887. As commissioner of British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-africa">Central Africa</a> from 1891 he fought the Yao and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> chiefs around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-malawi">Lake Malawi</a>, founding Nyasaland, and as special commissioner in Uganda he negotiated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buganda-agreement">Buganda Agreement</a> of 1900.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/harry-johnston.jpg" alt="Harry Johnston" width="320" height="443" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Elliott &amp; Fry · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Harry_Johnston.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under the Buganda Agreement of 1900, negotiated by <b>Sir Harry Johnston</b> with the regents of the young Kabaka Daudi Chwa II, led by Katikkiro Apolo Kagwa, Britain recognised the kabaka, the Lukiiko and the chiefs, and distributed large estates of freehold mailo land to the chiefs and the…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-buganda">Kingdom of Buganda · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>To block Portuguese claims, Britain declared the Shire Highlands a protectorate in 1889 and in 1891 proclaimed the British Central Africa Protectorate, under Commissioner <b>Harry Johnston</b> with his headquarters at Zomba.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-central-africa-protectorate">British Central Africa Protectorate</a></li><li><blockquote>The Jumbe signed a treaty with Britain in 1889 and cooperated with <b>Harry Johnston</b> for a time, but in the mid-1890s the British removed the last Jumbe and absorbed Nkhotakota into the British Central Africa Protectorate.</blockquote><a href="/state/jumbe-state-of-nkhotakota">Jumbe State of Nkhotakota</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1887 the British consul <b>Harry Johnston</b> lured him aboard a ship and deported him; he died in 1891.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-opobo">Kingdom of Opobo</a></li><li><blockquote>Makanjira resisted the British Central Africa Protectorate under <b>Harry Johnston</b>; his towns were attacked from 1891, Fort Maguire was built on his land, and by 1895 he had been driven out and the chiefdom brought under colonial rule.</blockquote><a href="/state/makanjira-chiefdom">Makanjira Chiefdom</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1907 the British Central Africa Protectorate, created by <b>Harry Johnston</b> in 1891 after wars against Yao slave traders, was renamed the Nyasaland Protectorate.</blockquote><a href="/state/nyasaland">Nyasaland</a></li></ul></section>
